   "source": [
    "# BIPN 162 - Fear Decoding\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "# Names\n",
    "\n",
    "- Jack Celaya\n",
    "- Sofia Lanaspa\n",
    "- Ehsun Yazdani\n",
    "\n",
    "# Abstract\n",
    "\n",
    "We will be doing option 4: re-analysis of existing data using methods discussed in class to ask a question not addressed in the original paper. In the paper, they recorded single neurons in the VP while rats got different tastants and were trained to associate saccharin (normally palatable) with a negative outcome. Over time, saccharin became aversive. By aligning neural activity with licking and looking at how firing patterns changed, they showed how VP neurons tracked palatability and learning. They used PCA to reduce data complexity and visualize how responses shifted with learning.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Research Question"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "How do ventral pallidum neural ensembles multiplex information about cued probilistic threats such as danger, uncertainty, saftey, and the anticipation or delivery of unsignaled background rewards, and can machine learning classifiers, such as Support Vector Machine, Random Forest, or Logistic Regression, reliabily decode these distinct or combined neural states from short temporal epochs of population activity?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Background and Prior Work"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Prior Work: The Ventral Pallidum (VP) is a critical subcortical structure within the basal ganglia, recognized for its central role in processing reward and pleasure. Its neurons robustly respond to natural rewards and drugs of abuse, making it a focal point for studies on motivation and addiction. However, emerging evidence reveals that the VP's role extends beyond pleasure to include the processing of aversive stimuli and the regulation of defensive behaviors. This positions the VP as a crucial hub for integrating information about both positive and negative outcomes to guide adaptive behavior. To study this, researchers often use learning paradigms like fear conditioning, where a neutral stimulus becomes associated with an aversive outcome. Understanding how VP neural populations encode and update the value of such cues is essential for a complete picture of its function in motivated behavior. Paper & Method Description: The dataset for this re-analysis originates from the study, "Ventral pallidum neurons dynamically signal relative threat," by Moaddab, Ray and McDannald (2021), published in Communications Biology. This project leverages machine learning techniques to decode the complex neural representations of threat signaling from a high-dimensional dataset of VP activity recorded in that study."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Hypothesis\n"
   ]
  },
